{Step 1: Transforming a given map} Suppose we have a map $f\colon Y\rightarrow X$ satisfying the monotonicity and continuity conditions. Since $I\subseteq Y$ for any $I\in\mathsf{RIdl}(Y,<)$, we thus define a map $\overline{f}\colon \mathsf{RIdl}(Y,<)\rightarrow X$ as sending $I\mapsto\!\operatorname*{\,\ThisStyle{\ensurestackMath{ 
			\bigsqcup\stackengine{-0pt}{\!}{\SavedStyle\!^{\mathord{\uparrow}}}{O}{l}{F}{T}{S}}}\,}_{q\in I}f(q)$.

		Conversely, suppose we have a map $\overline{f}\colon \mathsf{RIdl}(Y,<)\rightarrow X$. We then define $f\colon Y\rightarrow X$ as sending $q\mapsto \overline{f}(I_q)$ That this definition of $f$ also satisfies the required properties is a consequence of the fact that $I_q=\!\operatorname*{\,\ThisStyle{\ensurestackMath{ 
			\bigsqcup\stackengine{-0pt}{\!}{\SavedStyle\!^{\mathord{\uparrow}}}{O}{l}{F}{T}{S}}}\,}_{q'<q}I_{q'}$. For \textbf{monotonicity}, observe that $I_q=\!\operatorname*{\,\ThisStyle{\ensurestackMath{ 
			\bigsqcup\stackengine{-0pt}{\!}{\SavedStyle\!^{\mathord{\uparrow}}}{O}{l}{F}{T}{S}}}\,}_{q'<q}I_{q'}$  means that $q'<q\implies I_{q'}\sqsubseteq I_{q}$. Hence, since $\overline{f}(I_{q'}\sqsubseteq I_{q})$ induces $\overline{f}(I_{q'})\sqsubseteq \overline{f}(I_{q})$ by functoriality, this gives $q'<q\implies f(q')\sqsubseteq f(q)$, as desired.
		\textbf{Continuity} follows immediately from the fact that maps preserve directed joins, and so
		$I_q=\!\operatorname*{\,\ThisStyle{\ensurestackMath{ 
			\bigsqcup\stackengine{-0pt}{\!}{\SavedStyle\!^{\mathord{\uparrow}}}{O}{l}{F}{T}{S}}}\,}_{q'<q}I_{q'}$ yields the identity $\overline{f}(I_{q})=\!\operatorname*{\,\ThisStyle{\ensurestackMath{ 
			\bigsqcup\stackengine{-0pt}{\!}{\SavedStyle\!^{\mathord{\uparrow}}}{O}{l}{F}{T}{S}}}\,}_{q'<q}\,\overline{f}(I_{q'})$.

