\label{lem:rational sandwich} For any pair of non-negative rationals $q,r\in Q$ such that $q<r$, and any positive natural number $a\in\mathbb{N}_+$, there exists a positive rational $s\in Q_+$ so that $q<s^a<r$.
		\begin{proof} Given $q,r\in Q$ such that $q<r$, denote $\epsilon:=\frac{r-q}{2}$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:monotonerationals}, we know that $\text{(---)}^a$ is unbounded on $Q$ --- thus there exists some $v\in Q$ such that $v^a>r$. Consider the function $\text{(---)}^a$ on the set of rationals from $0$ to $v$. For any two rationals $x,y$ such that $0\leq x<y\leq v$, we have:
			\begin{align*}
			y^a-x^a&=(y-x)\cdot (y^{a-1}+y^{a-2}\cdot x + \dots + y\cdot x^{a-2}+x^{a-1})\\
			&\leq (y-x)\cdot (v^{a-1}+v^{a-2}\cdot v + \dots  + v\cdot v^{a-2}+v^{a-1})\\
			& = (y-x)\cdot a\cdot v^{a-1}
			\text{.}
			\end{align*}

			\noindent By the Archimedean property (Fact~\ref{fact:archproperty}), there exists some $M\in\mathbb{N}$ such that $\frac{1}{M}<\frac{\epsilon}{a\cdot v^a}$. Denoting $s_i:=\frac{i\cdot v}{M}$, it is clear that:
			\[\displaystyle\bigcup_{0\leq i\leq M}[s_i,s_{i+1}]=[0,v]\]
			and that $s_{i+1}-s_i = \frac{v\cdot (i+1-i)}{M}=\frac{v}{M}<\frac{\epsilon}{a\cdot v^{a-1}}$, and so $s_{i+1}^a-s_i^a < \epsilon$.
			\begin{comment}

                                                                             \end{comment}

			Next, one easily verifies that since:
			\begin{itemize}
				\item 		 $s_0^a=0\leq q$;
				\item $s_M^a=v^a>r>q$; and
				\item  $\{s_i^a\}$ is strictly monotone in $i\in \{0,1.,...,M\}$;
			\end{itemize}
			there thus exists a unique $j$ such that $s_j^a\leq q<s_{j+1}^a$.
