\label{prop:onesidedrealexpI}
		For Dedekind $x\geq 1$, the exponentiation by arbitrary rationals can be extended to one-sided exponents, giving exponentiation maps
		\[
		[1,\infty)\times\overrightarrow{[-\infty,\infty]} \to \overrightarrow{[0,\infty]}
		\text{ and }
		[1,\infty)\times\overleftarrow{[-\infty,\infty]} \to \overleftarrow{[0,\infty]}
		\text{.}
		\]
		Each map is unique subject to being monoid homomorphism for $\zeta\in \overrightarrow{[-\infty,\infty]}$ or  $\zeta\in \overleftarrow{[-\infty,\infty]}$,
		and satisfies the Basic Equations~\eqref{eq:BE1}--\eqref{eq:BE3}.

\label{thm:zetaDed}
		For Dedekind exponent $\zeta\geq 0$ and one-sided base $x$, we can define exponentiation maps
		\[
		\overrightarrow{(0,\infty]}\times [0, \infty) \to \overrightarrow{(0,\infty]}
		\text{ and }
		\overleftarrow{[0, \infty)}\times [0, \infty) \to \overleftarrow{[0, \infty)}
		\]
		such that, for $x$ Dedekind, $(L_x)^\zeta = L_{x^\zeta}$ and $(R_{x})^\zeta = R_{x^\zeta}$. The Basic Equations hold for these maps.

\label{thm:zetaReal}
		For one-sided $x\geq 1$, the exponentiation by non-negative rational exponents (Subsection~\ref{subsec:RationalExpReals}) can be extended to one-sided exponents, giving exponentiation maps
		\[
		\overrightarrow{[1,\infty]}\times\overrightarrow{[0,\infty]} \to \overrightarrow{[1,\infty]}
		\text{ and }
		\overleftarrow{[1,\infty]}\times\overleftarrow{[0,\infty]} \to \overleftarrow{[1,\infty]}
		\text{.}
		\]
		They satisfy the Basic Equations~\eqref{eq:BE1}--\eqref{eq:BE3}.

One can also define a logarithm map with base $b\in (0,1)$ in terms of the previous $\log$ map, as follows:
		\begin{align*}
		\log\colon(0,1)&\times (0,\infty)\rightarrow \mathbb{R}\\
		(\, b &,y\, ) \longmapsto -\log(\,{b^{-1}},y\,).
		\end{align*}
